        r"""<p>The dynamic observables representing the kinematic properties of these wave functions are represented by self-adjoint operators acting on the dense domains of the Hilbert space. The spatial distribution of the particle is resolved by <strong>The Position Operator (\(\hat{x}\))</strong>, which acts as a multiplicative operator on the wave function \( \psi(x, t) \), defining the localized coordinates of the carrier wave. The conjugate momentum is governed by the derivative operator \( \hat{p} = -i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial x} \), which satisfies the canonical commutation relation \( [\hat{x}, \hat{p}] = i\hbar \) across all coordinates. By defining these canonical operators, the theory guarantees that the position and momentum satisfy the Heisenberg uncertainty relation, establishing the quantum-mechanical limits of simultaneous measurements.</p>"""
